----
-title: "A cloned TRef needs the source file's ProcessID records, not just the copied baskets"
-kind: "gotcha"
-summary: "A clone must copy the source file's ProcessID records, because a split collection's TRef record names its process only by that index, and TRef::GetObject returns nothing without it."
-tags: [tref, processid, clonetree, roottest, trees]
-created: "2026-11-21T05:07:20.620Z"
-updated: "2026-11-21T05:07:20.620Z"
----
-A `TRef` written inside a split collection (a `TClonesArray` member of a class, e.g. roottest's `tref_test_pid::fRef`) is stored as a record whose `fPID` field is an **index among the `ProcessID` records of the file that record was written to** - not a process name and not a session number. `TTree::CloneTree` copies those rows byte for byte, so a clone's file must be given the *source* file's `ProcessID` records (ROOT's clone ends up with the identical UUID, e.g. `ProcessID0` titled `0b183a69-...`); otherwise `TRef::GetObject` reads back null even though the entry beside it holds the object.
-
-## How the pieces fit
-
-```mermaid
-graph LR
-  A[source file<br/>ProcessID0 = uuid] -->|read rows| B[ClonedSlot<br/>rows keep fPID = 0]
-  A -->|_directory/backing._source| C[_source_uuid]
-  B --> D[_renumbered]
-  C --> D
-  D -->|WriteProcessID uuid| E[new file<br/>ProcessID0 = same uuid]
-  D -->|fPID rewritten to new index| F[writer streams the record]
-  E --> F
-  F --> G[TRef::GetObject finds the object]
-```
-
-## Where it lives in xrdroot
-
-- `src/xrdroot/pyroot/trees/objects.py` - `ClonedSlot.columns()` now calls `Kind.written()` and then `_carried_processes(keys, members, columns, source)`, which for every whole-`TRef` member rewrites each row's `fPID` through `_renumbered()` -> `_source_uuid()` -> `TFile::WriteProcessID`. `_objects()` streams a clone's rows with `_described_record()` (`wbranch._ref_record`) instead of `tref_record()`, which needs a live `TRef` object.
-- `src/xrdroot/pyroot/trees/copying.py` - `_directory(backing)` is how the source *file* is found: an `xrdroot.tree.TTree` keeps the `Source` it was read through (`getattr(backing, "_source")`), not the file, so it matches that source against `xrdroot.session.gROOT.files`. `_slots()` passes the result as `ClonedSlot(..., source=...)`.
-- `src/xrdroot/pyroot/core/files.py` - `TFile::WriteProcessID(pid)` accepts either a `TProcessID` or the UUID **string** another file titled the record with.
-- Paths that do *not* work and that the fix routes around: `wbranch.ReferenceWhole.pack` / `_ref_record` (copies `fPID` verbatim, writes no record) and `objects._named_processes` (only handles live `TRef` objects, which a clone never has for these rows).
-
-## Checking it
-
-```bash
-cd /tmp/rootsrc-6.40b/roottest/root/io/tree
-export PYTHONPATH=/tmp/r28/w01/src   # any checkout
-PY=/path/to/.venv/bin/python
-for s in first create-r create-rc test-rc; do
-  $PY -m xrdroot root -e '(void)gROOT->ProcessLine(".L tref_test_pid.C+")' \
-      -e "#define STAGE \"$s\"" -q -b run.C
-done
-$PY -m xrdroot ls -l tref_test_pid_rc.root   # must list ProcessID0 before TTree T
-```
-
-The `-e ... -q -b file.C` word order is `root.exe`'s; `xrdroot run` does not take them. The oracle for comparison: `~/.local/root-oracle/bin/root -l -b -q -e ... run.C`, then `rootls -l` on both files. The harness measures this with `python -m tools.rootsuite run --root /tmp/rootsrc-6.40b --jobs 1 --only 'roottest/root/io/tree/*'`; those tests share one working directory, so always `--jobs 1`.
-
-## What is still open in that test family
-
-- `test-rcr`, `test-rcrr`, `test-rcrr3`: only `TTree::Show`'s text differs (`src/xrdroot/pyroot/trees/printing.py`).
-- `test-rcr2`, `test-rcrr2`, `test-rcrr123`: `CloneTree(0)`+`Fill` writes the *values read* for a collection holding whole `TRef`s (see `ClonedSlot._live`), not the live objects `SetBranchAddress` holds, so `r2`'s references find the `N` object rather than the `r1` one. Lifting that guard fixes these three but breaks `r1` in the other three, because `src/xrdroot/pyroot/trees/branches.py::read_referenced` reads *every* collection of the entry and the last one filed wins - ROOT instead reads the branch its `TRefTable` names for each identifier.
-- `N.fBits` is written as `24` where ROOT keeps `50331672` (`kIsOnHeap|kNotDeleted`, stripped by `objects.py::_member`'s `& ~HEAP_BITS`), and the session's first process is numbered `0` where ROOT numbers it `1` (`fUniqueID` `9` vs `0x01000009`, `src/xrdroot/pyroot/core/references.py`).
